azgor wrote:
> Greetings!  This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC
> channel on freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be
> present.

Here we go, one at a time:

> Rodney ideas[2]: Eating garlic should temporarily protect you from the
> special attack of foocubi.

Cool.


> Rodney ideas[3]: High level spellcaster monsters, on finding a cursed
> scroll of magic mapping can, when they read it, randomise the current
> level.

Not cool: what happens to objects already dropped?  What happens to
traps already generated?  What if it's a level with a special feature
(magic portal, extra up or down stairs)?  A special NPC (Oracle)?  What
if it's a special level (Castle)?  This is way too big a can of worms,
and if you put it in the hands of a monster it could happen repeatedly,
which is bad.  Anyway, this is Nethack, not Angband; level persistence
is part of what makes the game what it is.


> Rodney ideas[4]: Magic lantern. Price as magic lamp. When lit, a normal
> lamp - but when a square becomes visible, it sometimes causes an image
> of a monster to appear there.. You get one when you wish for a magic
> lamp. Rarely occuring.

I don't get it.

> Rodney ideas[5]: If you fail to cast a spell by just a few percent,
> instead of doing nothing you would get an effect similar to reading a
> scroll while confused (the scroll-based spells could have  the same
> confused effect).  Casting spells while confused could have the same
> confused result with "just failed" meaning the spell casts normally.

Not cool: some confused scroll effects are, in some cases, better than
the real ones (especially with taming).  For this to be balanced, the
failure effect would always have to be considerably worse than the
success, or the meaning of "knowing" a spell would be overturned.


> Rodney ideas[6]: If unskilled in flail, nunchaku should periodically
> deal a small amount of damage to the user.  For a few percent of these
> self-inflicted hits, the player should also be stunned, but only if
> male. ;)

Not quite cool: It damages sex parity.  If you have a player who finds
early nunchaku, then this could make it much better to be female than
male.  I'm all for differentiating the sexes in meaningful ways, but
not in ways that make one obviously better than the other.


> Rodney ideas[7]: Sunsword should turn Trolls to stone.

Cool.  Although you'd think Trollbane would have an effect like this.
(I know I know, old YANI)


> Rodney ideas[8]: "Unidentified items" as a type of object to be removed
> from a container (Note: there's a patch that does this at
> http://nh.gmuf.com/#itemcat which also includes a few more item
> categories)

Cool.


> Rodney ideas[9]: !oBooze should count as an aperitif and consume
> nutrition when quaffed.

Not sure.  What if that's enough to take the player into starvation,
what's the cause of death?  Anyway, this isn't even very whimsical, so
I lean against it.


> Rodney ideas[10]: Gold-plated weapons - heavy, ineffective, but
> inherently corrosion-proof.  (Apologies to Zarel and Dirm for adding
> this in.)

Interesting.  Note that the ancient protection scrolls in Rogue covered
equipment with a "shimmering gold shield."  I don't know though, I
don't see any compelling reason to add this, especially with
erodeproofing in the game.


> Rodney ideas[11]: Stone nymph - teleports next to you, hands you a
> loadstone, takes your GoP or =oGS (if you've got them) and runs away on
> foot.

I don't get it.  Even if I did get it, that lodestone thing is
potentially drastically dangerous.


> Rodney ideas[12]: Water moccasins should have an increased chance of
> dropping water walking boots.

Not cool: effect too subtle, plus it further upsets item generation
balance.


> Rodney ideas[13]: when mimicing a pile of gold, armor should fall off.

Interesting.  Somewhat cool.


> Rodney ideas[14]: Cursed amulets of lifesaving have a 50% chance of not
> working, 49% chance of working, and 1% chance of working only after you
> get your player information and see the tombstone.

If I remember correctly, part of the process of generating the
end-of-game report is destructive, so more coding would be required to
do this than you think.  I appreciate the spirit behind this one,
however.  I think the amulet shouldn't be made inoperative sometimes,
however: the bad effect from all cursed amulets comes from the
inability to remove it, not from modifying its workings.  (Same goes
for rings, weapons and armor.)


> Rodney ideas[15]: The ability to mark a square. You can add some text
> (like an engraving) to a square which A) never erodes B) has no in-game
> effect C) takes no game time. Being able to select the new tile color
> would be useful for detecting markings. Analogous to naming an item.

Could be interesting, but coming up with the interface aspects of this
would not be trivial.


> Rodney ideas[16]: A stripling is a male youth.  Valkyries are female.

Then what should a low-level Valk be called?


> Rodney ideas[17]: When hallucinating - you may #twoweapon your primary
> weapon in both your primary and secondary slots. Any attack made this
> way has a 1% chance per hit of being a vorpal attack - randomly either
> on the monster, or on the player.

Too capricious and narrow-focused.  Nethack is actually not a game
about random death.


> Rodney ideas[18]: Reading a (blank?) scroll while hallucination should
> have random effects.  As if reading a random scroll of the same BUC
> while confused.  This should not identify the scroll.

Hallucination shouldn't be made too useful.  Also note, this would mean
that reading cursed blank scrolls while hallucinating is always a bad
move (potential for instadeath).


> Rodney ideas[19]: When puddings split their max HP gets cut in half. If
> this would result in a max hp of zero (or one?) they die instead of
> splitting.
> Rodney ideas[20]: That's a silly idea. Make it a conduct - all fixed.
> Rodney ideas[21]: Or perhaps people should stop whining about pudding
> farming and/or elbereth

Of Nos 19, 20 and 21, 19 is by far the best here.  Pudding farming is
an abuse -- there, I said it, and I'm more confident of it this time.
It is a flaw in Nethack's design an order of magnitude worse than nurse
dancing or polypiling, because it breaks item rarity.  The best
solution, however, is merely to disallow black puddings from dropping
random stuff.

On the note about Elbereth: there's a source mechanism to easily remove
that feature from a build of the game.  The dev team evidently saw
concerns about that coming.


> Rodney ideas[22]: If a wand or spell does not actually ask for
> directions (due to not having enough charges, PW, or the spell
> failing), require the player to hit space so the following doesn't
> happen. Z,a,6, You are stunned by the floating eye, you are hit!, you
> are hit! You die...

Not cool in the case of wands.  Wresting the last charge out of a wand
of wishing is hard enough as it is!


> Rodney ideas[23]: nethack should talk like a pirate on Talk Like a
> Pirate Day.  "Do ye be wantin' yer possessions identified?"

Certainly a worthy whimsy.  Only problem is, it could potentially be a
very large amount of text to rewrite, cluttering up the code
tremendously in the service of a joke that would be seen one day in
365.

- John H.



